### Layer 1 — `cli/explain.go`

#### Pre-flight message

In `generateCheckpointSummary`, before calling `generateCheckpointAISummary`,
print the size up front. This grounds the wait:

```go
fmt.Fprintf(errW, "Generating checkpoint summary... (transcript: %s)\n",
    humanizeBytes(len(scopedTranscript)))
```

Tiny `humanizeBytes` helper (KB / MB / single decimal). No size buckets, no
heuristics — just the actual number, so the user can calibrate their own
patience.

#### Periodic progress reporter

Replaces the single delayed "Still waiting…" line with a recurring update.
Cadence is sparse so it doesn't spam:

| Elapsed | Message |
| --- | --- |
| 10s | `…still working (10s elapsed)` |
| 30s | `…still working (30s elapsed). Press Ctrl+C to cancel.` |
| 1m, 2m, 5m | `…still working (Nm elapsed). Press Ctrl+C to cancel.` |
| every 5m after | (continues at 5m intervals) |

Implemented as a small ticker-driven goroutine:

```go
// startProgressReporter prints "…still working" updates at increasing
// intervals until the returned cancel function fires.
func startProgressReporter(errW io.Writer) (cancel func()) {
    if errW == nil { return func() {} }
    done := make(chan struct{})
    go func() {
        start := time.Now()
        // checkpoints in seconds; -1 sentinel means "from here on, every 5m"
        checkpoints := []int{10, 30, 60, 120, 300}
        idx := 0
        for {
            var wait time.Duration
            if idx < len(checkpoints) {
                wait = time.Duration(checkpoints[idx])*time.Second - time.Since(start)
            } else {
                wait = 5 * time.Minute
            }
            if wait < time.Second { wait = time.Second }
            select {
            case <-time.After(wait):
                elapsed := time.Since(start).Round(time.Second)
                hint := ""
                if elapsed >= 30*time.Second { hint = " Press Ctrl+C to cancel." }
                fmt.Fprintf(errW, "  …still working (%s elapsed).%s\n", elapsed, hint)
                idx++
            case <-done:
                return
            }
        }
    }()
    return func() { close(done) }
}
```

`generateCheckpointSummary` calls it after the pre-flight message and
`defer cancel()`s it.

#### Timeout strategy: TTY-gated

`generateCheckpointAISummary` decides whether to wrap the parent context with
a deadline:

```go
func generateCheckpointAISummary(ctx context.Context, scopedTranscript []byte, ...) (*checkpoint.Summary, error) {
    timeout := resolveSummaryTimeout(errW) // see below
    runCtx := ctx
    var cancel context.CancelFunc
    if timeout > 0 {
        runCtx, cancel = context.WithTimeout(ctx, timeout)
        defer cancel()
    }
    // ...existing call into generateTranscriptSummary using runCtx...
}

// resolveSummaryTimeout returns the effective timeout, or 0 for "no deadline".
// Precedence: explicit settings override > non-TTY safety default > TTY no-deadline.
func resolveSummaryTimeout(errW io.Writer) time.Duration {
    if t := settings.SummaryTimeout(); t > 0 {
        return t
    }
    if !isInteractiveStderr(errW) {
        return defaultNonTTYTimeout // 5 * time.Minute
    }
    return 0 // interactive: user Ctrl+C is the timeout
}

func isInteractiveStderr(errW io.Writer) bool {
    f, ok := errW.(*os.File)
    if !ok { return false }
    return term.IsTerminal(int(f.Fd()))
}
```

Rationale:

- **Interactive** (default): user sees pre-flight + progress + can Ctrl+C
  whenever. No hardcoded duration overrides their judgment.
- **Non-interactive** (CI, scripts, `entire explain --generate | cat`,
  redirected stderr): no human is watching, so we need a hang-guard. 5 minutes
  is generous enough that even very large transcripts complete; tight enough
  that a truly hung CLI doesn't wedge a CI job indefinitely.
- **Settings override** wins everywhere — power users can opt into a hard
  interactive cap or extend the non-TTY default.

